A minimum spanning tree (MST) or minimum weight spanning tree for a weighted, connected, and undirected graph is a spanning tree (no cycles and connects all vertices) that has minimum weight. The weight of a spanning tree is the sum of all edges in the tree.  Below are the steps for finding MST using Kruskal's algorithm:Sort all the edges in a non-decreasing order of their weight. Pick the smallest edge. Check if it forms a cycle with the spanning tree formed so far. If the cycle is not formed, include this edge. Else, discard it.  It uses the Disjoint Sets to detect cycles.Repeat step 2 until there are (V-1) edges in the spanning tree.It picks the minimum weighted edge at first and the maximum weighted edge at last. Thus we can say that it makes a locally optimal choice in each step in order to find the optimal solution. Hence this is a Greedy Algorithm.Illustration:The graph contains 9 vertices and 14 edges. So, the minimum spanning tree formed will be having (9 - 1) = 8 edges.Try It Yourself
